const root=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="ALIGN — Evidence-led job discovery / Independent case study" description="An independent, AI-assisted UX and interaction-design concept exploring job conditions, inspectable evidence, private comparison and explicit demo application review.">
<div id="top"><SiteHeader /><main id="main" class="container case-study align-case" tabindex="-1">
<div class="align-opening"><header class="case-hero"><a class="case-back" href={`${root}#work`}>← Back to selected work</a><p class="micro">Independent product concept · AI-assisted · 2026</p><h1>ALIGN</h1><p class="case-deck">Understand the opportunity before committing.</p><p class="case-scope">A responsive job-discovery prototype connecting listing facts, preferences and unknowns before application review.</p><p class="case-role"><span class="micro">My contribution</span>Product direction and iterative feedback. AI-assisted design exploration, documentation, implementation and checks.</p></header>
<figure class="align-figure align-hero"><img src={discovery.src} width={discovery.width} height={discovery.height} alt="ALIGN M06 mobile and desktop discovery layouts with a fictional role, conditions and Decision Brief." loading="eager" decoding="async" /><figcaption><span>M06 discovery · Original responsive browser-frame capture.</span><span>Historical design artifact; the linked candidate demo uses R05. Fictional data throughout.</span><a href={discovery.src}>Open full-size discovery artifact</a></figcaption></figure><div class="align-entry"><a class="text-link" href={`${root}prototypes/align/`}>Explore the ALIGN prototype <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#decisions">Explore the design decisions <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></div>
<CaseOverview items={[
{label:'Intended users',value:'Job seekers comparing conditions and evidence before deciding what to pursue.'},
{label:'Scope',value:'Candidate and employer design exploration; a working candidate web prototype.'},
{label:'Contribution',value:'Product direction and feedback by Suleyman; AI-assisted design and engineering.'},
{label:'Status',value:'Independent concept. No market validation, real employer delivery or hiring outcomes.'},
]} />
<DecisionIndex items={[
{ title:'Pair orientation with inspectable evidence', explanation:'A concise summary sits beside detailed sources and unknowns, preserving caveats without asking a candidate to start in a long ledger.', target:'decisions' },
{ title:'Treat owner review as a signal, not user research', explanation:'The internal walkthrough led to clearer seniority and confirmation language; independent comprehension and task outcomes remain untested.', target:'iterations' },
{ title:'Make the demo boundary explicit', explanation:'The application flow ends in a local receipt that says employer delivery was not sent.', target:'implementation' },
]} />
<nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Case study sections"><p class="micro">In this case study</p><ul><li><a href="#premise">The premise</a></li><li><a href="#journey">The candidate journey</a></li><li><a href="#decisions">Decision Brief</a></li><li><a href="#iterations">Refinement</a></li><li><a href="#implementation">Interaction quality</a></li><li><a href="#reflection">Outcome & reflection</a></li></ul></nav>
<section id="premise" class="align-section"><header><p class="micro">01 / Product premise</p><h2>Conditions before commitments.</h2></header><div><p>The starting hypothesis was that candidates need to understand what aligns with their preferences, what differs, and what remains unknown. It came from the project brief, not interviews or a measured market problem.</p><p>Early AI-generated stakeholder scenarios helped generate questions. They were synthetic exploration, not real participants or employer partnerships. A listing fact, a candidate declaration and verified evidence need different meanings in the interface.</p></div></section>
<section id="journey" class="align-section align-wide"><header><p class="micro">02 / Information architecture</p><h2>Four intentions. A deliberate path.</h2></header><ol class="align-journey"><li><h3>Discover</h3><p>Explore one role. Save, pass, undo or inspect details using explicit controls.</p></li><li><h3>Search</h3><p>Investigate through filters and stable results. Desktop supports list/detail review.</p></li><li><h3>Saved</h3><p>Organize private notes and compare the same criteria across a shortlist.</p></li><li><h3>Applications</h3><p>Review a fictional payload and return to a local receipt with an explicit delivery status.</p></li></ol><figure class="align-figure"><img src={search.src} width={search.width} height={search.height} alt="Original M06 desktop Search interface showing filters, results and role details side by side." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption><span>M06 desktop Search · Original historical capture.</span><a href={search.src}>Open full-size Search artifact</a></figcaption></figure><p class="align-boundary"><strong>Saving is private.</strong> It does not apply, express interest or change an employer’s review status. Employer design artifacts remain a separate exploration.</p></section>
<section id="decisions" class="align-section align-wide"><header><p class="micro">03 / The defining component</p><h2>An explanation you can inspect.</h2></header><div class="align-decision-pair"><div><h3>Summary + evidence</h3><p>Quick chips make work arrangement and pay recognizable. A summary gives orientation; detailed evidence keeps requirements, declarations and unknowns available for scrutiny.</p><p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> concise copy could conceal caveats. The chosen structure preserves uncertainty and avoids declaring a winner or predicting eligibility.</p><dl class="align-evidence"><div><dt>Self-declared</dt><dd>A candidate statement, not verified work.</dd></div><div><dt>Unknown</dt><dd>Evidence is missing; experience is not inferred.</dd></div><div><dt>Potential gap</dt><dd>An explicit declaration of absent experience, distinct from an unanswered field.</dd></div></dl></div><figure class="align-figure"><img src={summary.src} width={summary.width} height={summary.height} alt="Historical M05 summary Decision Brief showing preference chips, qualification evidence, potential gaps and unknown information." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption><span>M05 Summary + evidence · Original alternative; fixtures and wording evolved later.</span><a href={summary.src}>Open full-size summary alternative</a></figcaption></figure></div><details class="align-alternative"><summary>Inspect the original evidence-ledger alternative</summary><p>The ledger keeps criteria and sources together, but repeats information and becomes long on a phone. These were design alternatives, not an A/B test.</p><figure class="align-figure"><img src={ledger.src} width={ledger.width} height={ledger.height} alt="Original M05 evidence-ledger design alternative with criterion-level evidence and unknowns." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption><span>M05 Evidence ledger · Preserved historical alternative.</span><a href={ledger.src}>Open full-size ledger alternative</a></figcaption></figure></details></section>
<section id="iterations" class="align-section align-wide"><header><p class="micro">04 / Owner review and iteration</p><h2>Make uncertainty and consequences visible.</h2></header><p>An internal walkthrough used the project owner’s chat responses and screenshots. Prior product knowledge limits those observations. There were no independent participants or task timings; the mobile walkthrough stopped before completion.</p><div class="align-iterations"><article><h3>Missing seniority</h3><p><strong>Observation:</strong> the owner could interpret conditions but could not identify the sought experience level.</p><p><strong>Response:</strong> state “Not specified in this listing.” Do not infer years from a job title.</p></article><article><h3>Comparison vocabulary</h3><p><strong>Observation:</strong> the owner requested arrangement indicators while comparing roles.</p><p><strong>Response:</strong> reuse labeled Remote, Hybrid and On-site chips as static facts. No speed improvement was measured.</p></article><article><h3>Confirmation meaning</h3><p><strong>Observation:</strong> the owner’s explanation mixed demo awareness with an expectation of delivery verification.</p><p><strong>Response:</strong> lead with the local result and “Employer delivery: Not sent.”</p></article></div><div class="align-artifact-pair"><figure class="align-figure"><img src={comparison.src} width={comparison.width} height={comparison.height} alt="Historical R03 responsive comparison artifact using consistent arrangement labels and criteria." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption><span>R03 comparison · Original historical capture.</span><a href={comparison.src}>Open full-size comparison artifact</a></figcaption></figure><figure class="align-figure"><img src={receipt.src} width={receipt.width} height={receipt.height} alt="Historical R03 local demo receipt explicitly stating employer delivery was not sent." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption><span>R03 receipt · A local browser record, not employer delivery.</span><a href={receipt.src}>Open full-size receipt artifact</a></figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section id="implementation" class="align-section"><header><p class="micro">05 / Interaction and engineering</p><h2>Preserve meaning through the journey.</h2></header><div><p>R04 refined focus recovery, linked errors and responsive fixed bars. R05 addressed private draft loss during view updates, stale Undo replacing newer preferences, and save feedback that could imply persistence after a failed write.</p><p>The resulting prototype uses explicit controls and shared evidence rules across discovery, details and comparison. The portfolio copy opens in memory-only preview mode, so its examples reset on reload and cannot alter the original ALIGN workspace.</p><details class="align-alternative"><summary>Documented checks and their limits</summary><p>The retained R05 report records 42 regression tests and 272 normal/enlarged-text layout cases. These are historical engineering counts, not participant metrics. Enlarged text was simulated; the iframe audit retained an unresolved console diagnostic. Physical-device, native-zoom and screen-reader evaluation remain incomplete.</p></details><a class="text-link" href={`${root}prototypes/align/`}>Try the candidate journey <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
<section id="reflection" class="align-section align-wide"><header><p class="micro">06 / Outcome and reflection</p><h2>A coherent prototype. Questions still to test.</h2></header><div class="align-outcomes"><article><h3>Created</h3><p>A responsive candidate prototype, employer design exploration, evidence states and preserved visual alternatives.</p></article><article><h3>Demonstrated</h3><p>Connected exploration, comparison and explicit local confirmation, with implementation checks within their documented scope.</p></article><article><h3>Unresolved</h3><p>Usefulness beyond three fictional listings, independent comprehension, assistive-technology behavior and real delivery infrastructure.</p></article></div><p>The central lesson is that information helps only when its source and consequences stay clear. The next evaluation should ask fresh reviewers to distinguish unknown evidence from an explicit gap and predict what confirmation will do.</p><p class="source-note">Product direction and iterative feedback: Suleyman Koncagul. Design exploration, documentation, implementation and checks were AI-assisted. No launched marketplace, real employer participation, independent hand-coding of every component, or hiring impact is claimed.</p></section>
<NextProject previous={{ slug: 'apple-ring', title: 'Apple Ring', description: 'An independent concept exploring device handoff, missing information, and shared interaction states.' }} />
</main><SiteFooter /></div></BaseLayout>
